#
# dash's printf is the POSIX one and has neither of the two extensions the
# other three share, which makes it alone on both.
#
# `%q` is not a conversion here: the format is written up to it and the run
# fails. And `\xHH` in the *format* is not an escape — bash, zsh and ksh93 all
# write the byte, dash writes the backslash, the x and the digits. A script
# reaching for either is writing for three shells out of four.
#
# ---- which dash this is measured against ----
#
# Upstream dash 0.5.12, which is the build every `dash 0.5.12` comment under
# interp/ and dialect/dash names. **Debian and Ubuntu patch this file's
# subject**, so this file will not go strict on a Debian-family runner and
# that is not a defect in the shell:
#
#	printf 'a\eZ' | od -An -tx1
#
#	Apple dash-16 (macOS /bin/dash)   61 5c 65 5a   the two characters
#	Alpine dash 0.5.12-r2            61 5c 65 5a   the two characters
#	Debian dash 0.5.12-12            61 1b 5a      an ESC
#
# Measured 2026-09-16. Two builds of the *same upstream version* disagree,
# which is what says it is the distribution's patch rather than a version
# this file is behind on. Matching Debian here would make cmd/dash wrong
# against upstream and wrong on the machine these cases were written on.
#
# It cannot be read off the column header either, and that is the other half
# of it: dash answers no version probe in any spelling, so the report prints
# `could not determine` and has nothing to raise a WRONG BUILD banner from —
# the banner the bash, zsh and ksh93 columns carry for exactly this. See
# #2291.

( printf '[%q]\n' 'a b' ) 2> /dev/null
printf 'percent q -> %s\n' "$( [ $? -ne 0 ] && printf nonzero || printf zero )"

printf 'hex in the format [\x41\x42]\n'
printf 'hex in a %%b operand [%b]\n' '\x41\x42'

# The escapes dash does have, for the same reason the core file asks the ones
# every shell shares: this file is about the two it lacks rather than about
# printf being different here.
printf 'octal in the format [\101\102]\n'
printf 'octal in a %%b operand [%b]\n' '\0101\0102'
printf 'a tab [\t] and a backslash [\\]\n'

# A numeric operand with a trailing blank. dash writes the number it read and
# calls the operand "not completely converted", which is its own sentence for
# a tail and not the one it gives an operand with no number in it at all --
# that is "expected numeric value", which the blank-only operand earns. bash
# has one sentence for both; zsh and ksh93 take the trailing blank silently.
# A leading blank is accepted everywhere and is the control.
printf '[%d]\n' ' 7'
printf 'a leading blank -> %s\n' "$?"
printf '[%d]\n' '7 '
printf 'a trailing blank -> %s\n' "$?"
printf '[%d]\n' ' '
printf 'a blank on its own -> %s\n' "$?"

# C's `#` at a value of nought, which is where ksh93 parts from every other
# shell and so cannot be asked in core/. C reads the alternate form off the
# *value*: the `0x` goes on a nonzero one, so a nought carries none, and an
# octal's precision is raised until there is a leading zero — which is a zero
# to write even where the precision had erased every digit. ksh93 reads the
# prefix off the digits instead, writing `0x0` for the first line here and
# nothing at all for the octal.
printf 'a hexadecimal nought [%#x] [%#X]\n' 0 0
printf 'through a precision [%#.2x] and a width [%#5x]\n' 0 0
printf 'and a zero fill [%#05x]\n' 0
printf 'an octal with nothing left [%#.0o] [%#5.0o]\n' 0 0

# The controls. `%#.0x` is where the two readings agree — no nonzero value
# and no digit for a prefix to stand in front of — `%#o` of nought is the
# octal already leading with its own zero, and the nonzero forms are the
# ordinary alternate form every shell writes the same way.
printf 'no value and no digits [%#.0x]\n' 0
printf 'an octal nought [%#o]\n' 0
printf 'nonzero [%#x] [%#o]\n' 255 255

# The `0` fill against the `0x` C's `#` wrote. C counts the prefix as part of
# the field, so the fill is the width less the `0x` and less the digits and
# `%#05x` of 7 is five characters. ksh93 pads the digits to the width and
# writes the prefix past it, which is seven — so these lines are here rather
# than in core/, which is the tier that has to agree in all five columns.
printf 'a hexadecimal [%#05x] [%#05X]\n' 7 255
printf 'a wider field [%#010x]\n' 255
printf 'the flags the other way round [%0#5x]\n' 7

# The width the prefix eats into, where C's fill goes empty two characters
# before the other reading's does. `%#05x` and `%#06x` of 65535 are both
# `0xffff` here and are `0x0ffff` and `0x00ffff` in ksh93, which is what
# makes the rule a subtraction that stops at nothing rather than a placement.
printf 'the prefix overflows [%#05x] [%#06x] and then does not [%#07x]\n' 65535 65535 65535

# A nought is the other reading and not this one: whether there is a prefix
# there at all is what #3024 settled, and the column that keeps one is the
# column that writes it past the width. Carried here so that a change to the
# fill cannot move it unnoticed.
printf 'a nought [%#05x]\n' 0

# Neither spelling of the escape character, at either site: this is the one
# column in the panel with no `\e` anywhere, and `printf '\e[1m'` writes the
# nine characters it was given. XCU gives a format the XSI escape set and
# nothing else, so the text is read as written rather than an escape being
# left out.
#
# Read as bytes, because an ESC written to a terminal is invisible and a case
# that compared rendered text could not tell an ESC from a backslash.
printf 'a\eZ:a\EZ' | od -An -c | tr -s ' '
printf '%b' 'a\eZ:a\EZ' | od -An -c | tr -s ' '
printf '\e[1mbold\e[0m' | od -An -c | tr -s ' '
